Apple is urging Canada to change a proposed law that it says could weaken the privacy and security protections built into its products.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/10/Black-Apple-Event-Logo.jpeg" alt="" width="2048" height="1152" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1068830" /><br/>
The legislation, Bill C-22, is intended to help police and intelligence agencies obtain digital information during investigations. According to <a href="https://ca.finance.yahoo.com/news/big-tech-mounts-last-ditch-110000009.html"><em>Bloomberg</em></a>, Apple, Google, and Meta are lobbying for changes as Canadian senators review the bill.<br/>
<br/>
At the center of the dispute is whether companies could be forced to change how their services work to help authorities access user data. The bill would allow the government to require companies to build tools for that purpose, with approval from Canada's Intelligence Commissioner.<br/>
<br/>
It could also require certain providers to keep records about users' connections for up to six months. This metadata would exclude the contents of messages, browsing history, and social media activities.<br/>
<br/>
Lawmakers have already <a href="https://www.parl.ca/DocumentViewer/en/45-1/bill/C-22/third-reading">added safeguards</a>. For example, a company could only be required to unlock encrypted data if it supplied the encryption and already has the information needed to unlock it. The bill also says companies cannot be forced to introduce certain security weaknesses into their services.<br/>
<br/>
Apple says those changes do not fully address its concerns. The company welcomed the government's revisions, but told <em>Bloomberg</em> that the bill would still undermine its ability to protect users' data. Apple <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/05/08/apple-warns-canada-bill-force-encryption-backdoors/">previously warned</a> that the legislation could let authorities demand backdoors.<br/>
<br/>
